WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:02.130
- [Narrator] Let's see if we can add

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:07.130
five and 2/5 to three and 4/5.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.340
Pause this video and see if you
can figure out what this is.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.020
All right, now let's do this together.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.400
We've had a little bit of practice

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.030
adding mixed numbers in the past.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.970
And so one way to think about it is,

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.970
you could view five and
2/5 as five plus 2/5.

00:00:22.985 --> 00:00:27.978
And then to that we're going
to be adding three and 4/5,

00:00:27.978 --> 00:00:32.364
which you could view as three plus 4/5.

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:33.829
And then you could just change the order

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:35.655
with which you are adding,

00:00:35.655 --> 00:00:36.619
and say, all right,

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.378
well, I could say five plus three,

00:00:39.378 --> 00:00:41.964
so that's five plus three,

00:00:41.964 --> 00:00:46.319
and then to that I could add 2/5 and 4/5,

00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:50.559
so, plus 2/5, plus 4/5.

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:51.900
Now what is that going to get me?

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.470
Well five plus three is
going to be equal to eight.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.907
Eight plus, and then if I have 2/5,

00:00:57.907 --> 00:01:00.000
and I add four more fifths to that,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.653
well now I'm going to have 6/5.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.140
Two of something plus
four of that something

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.230
is going to be six of that something,

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.460
and the something in this case are fifths.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.163
So now I'm going to have 6/5.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.567
So some of you might be tempted to say,

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.123
"Hey, isn't this just going to be equal to

00:01:15.123 --> 00:01:19.040
eight and 6/5?"

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.060
And you wouldn't be completely
wrong if you said that.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.300
But pause this video and think about

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.655
why this feels a little bit off.

00:01:26.655 --> 00:01:29.241
Well, the reason why this isn't standard,

00:01:29.241 --> 00:01:33.020
is the fractional part
of this mixed number 6/5

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.830
is greater than one.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:38.170
So there's a whole inside of this 6/5.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.060
So the standard way to do this is

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.537
see if we can break out that whole.

00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:43.600
Now what do I mean by that?

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.690
Well I could rewrite eight plus 6/5

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.550
as eight plus 6/5 is the same thing

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:55.550
as a whole, or 5/5 plus 1/5.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.930
And why is this useful?

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.030
Well 5/5 is the same thing as one.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.140
And so now I can say
this is going to be equal

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.040
to eight plus one whole is nine,

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.690
that's eight plus the 5/5,

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.350
and then what I have leftover is 1/5.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.030
So nine and 1/5.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.990
And so this is the direction

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.600
that people will traditionally go in.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.240
Now there's another way
that you could approach it,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.920
which is really the same idea,

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.140
we're just writing things
a little bit differently.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.900
We could write this as five and 2/5

00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:32.351
plus three and 4/5,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.770
and notice the way that I wrote it.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.270
I put all the fractions
in the fraction column,

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.210
I guess you could call it that way,

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.270
and I put all of our whole
numbers underneath each other,

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.880
and if I had multiple digits here,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.490
I would align them
according to place value.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.030
And then what we could do is we could say,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.270
okay 2/5 plus 4/5 is going to be 6/5.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.230
We could write 6/5 there.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.750
But we say, hey, there's
something a little bit

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.270
fishy about 6/5.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.862
That's really the same
thing as 5/5 plus 1/5,

00:03:04.862 --> 00:03:08.030
or you could say that's the
same thing as one and 1/5.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.410
6/5 is equal to one and 1/5,

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.100
and so what you could do is,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.020
you could write the 1/5 part
in the fractions column,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.340
and then the one well now you're
going to be regrouping that

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:20.560
into our whole numbers,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.040
so you put a one right over there.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.940
Notice, 2/5 plus 4/5 is one and 1/5,

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.570
which is the same thing as 6/5.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.510
And then you add the whole number part.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.370
One plus five plus three is nine.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.006
So you get nine and 1/5.

00:03:34.006 --> 00:03:35.330
But hopefully you realize

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.940
that these are really the same idea,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.230
just different way of writing things

